#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1440.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130216} :activity/id #uuid "c10a40bf-815b-59d3-8b54-688b2275327e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048736 :topic/id #uuid "b9fc7121-4ed8-5254-ad37-ccaadac27c0e" :topic/math-academy-id 2028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130216 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130459 :tutorial/content "Consider a plane lamina that occupies the region $D \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$ of mass $M$ where the center of mass is at $\\left(\\bar{x},\\bar{y}\\right).$\n\n![](images/4c/4c2c7e3525dfe8aea3cce69a7332ccf5ca82ddfe37657071ddef35650bcea383.png)\n\nWe know that the lamina has some moment of inertia $I_M$ about the line $l_M$ that passes through the center of mass and is perpendicular to the lamina.\n\n![](images/96/9638e9d47ef3c9b2fab47f39cf70e2ba07863c6e949755c8378e5737caa0a350.png)\n\nNow, if $l$ is a line parallel to $l_M,$ then the lamina also has moment of inertia about $l,$ say $I_l.$\n\n![](images/06/06819ca45bd176ff9d36b07c73574bc9f2911a7b0b96b5b916ba76f8606d2f60.png)\n\nGiven the value of $I_M,$ we can find $I_l$ using the **parallel axis theorem**, which states that\n\n$$\nI_l = I_M + d^2M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines.\n\nThe term $d^2M$ is the moment of inertia about $l$ of the lamina when its entire mass is concentrated at the center of mass.\n\n![](images/fb/fbab9fab43dd45fd80008704f406f94864cac57d636320d250dca86ff465f196.png)\n\nSo, our interpretation of the parallel axis theorem is the following:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about a parallel line }l}_{I_l}\n\\:=\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of }D \\ \\text{about the center of mass}}_{I_M}\n\\:+\\:\n\\underbrace{\\text{moment of inertia of point mass }M \\ \\text{about the parallel line } l}_{d^2M}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $d^2M$ is always positive, we have $I_M \\leq I_l$ for any line $l$ perpendicular to the lamina. In other words, the moment of inertia about the line through its center of mass is the *smallest* moment of inertia about any line in that direction." :tutorial/id #uuid "b1dda94b-0ea5-5ff2-a3d5-a406dfc9b95d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7332 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "aae6c762-bad6-59ad-bbac-4dd3947ca34e" :step/math-academy-id 43340 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130217}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130217 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128850 :question/id #uuid "915a7c2d-3797-5dfa-8d68-85e2546a23d6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11778"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de55bc-23b5-574c-9fab-33c4bca720fe" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048585}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431765}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis"} :step/id #uuid "a371a864-c197-5aa0-8d21-3cead8839e82" :step/math-academy-id 43341 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130262}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130218 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128851 :question/id #uuid "153b828d-545a-58fb-bc81-0d7311726a87" :question/math-academy-id "e-11779"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "850f196a-9607-56c2-b0a7-a00d4d5a4741" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326694} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372314} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500372}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia About a Perpendicular Axis Using Polar Coordinates"} :step/id #uuid "2f9433b3-3716-5cab-88dc-0c8106c423cf" :step/math-academy-id 43342 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130249}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130249 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128864 :question/id #uuid "72ccaeab-ad76-50c7-8b51-7736c5270521" :question/math-academy-id "e-12493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c5c774e2-7c3f-56a9-99b6-96aca1cd4f49" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284440} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372258} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500335}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distance Between Two Parallel Axes"} :step/id #uuid "942eea0b-0401-5ec3-980b-a17bb6c3cdaa" :step/math-academy-id 44997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129290 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128865 :question/id #uuid "131948d9-d533-5316-bcd3-b7859244892e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12494"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21f203c5-4866-5077-9ef9-0d2122581c4f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389490} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186431766}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Moment of Inertia of a Rectangular Plate About a Perpendicular Axis"} :step/id #uuid "e5790220-0fbc-5caf-b3ee-9039c4289a67" :step/math-academy-id 45003 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130218}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130262 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130480 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we saw that the moment of inertia about an axis $l$ of a plane lamina occupying the region $D$ with mass density function $\\lambda(x,y)$ is given by\n\n$$\nI_l = \\iint\\limits_D [r(x,y)]^2\\,\\lambda(x,y)  \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia of $D$ about the line through the center of mass $\\left(\\bar{x}, \\bar{y}\\right)$ and perpendicular to the lamina is given by\n\n$$\nI_M = \\iint\\limits_D \\left((x-\\bar{x})^2+(y-\\bar{y})^2\\right) \\lambda(x,y) \\:\\textrm{d}A.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a08f490-ccca-5a93-adb8-dbf845e4d4cc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7849 :tutorial/title "A Formula for the Moment of Inertia About the Line Through the Center of Mass"} :step/id #uuid "f23fbe74-8ade-5fcf-9513-af6482f642b2" :step/math-academy-id 45317 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186130250}}] :activity/title "The Parallel Axis Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186129370}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500404} :activity/id #uuid "a6c4c785-a86b-556d-b318-805973a4f043" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048736 :topic/id #uuid "b9fc7121-4ed8-5254-ad37-ccaadac27c0e" :topic/math-academy-id 2028} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500385 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25762675-e43f-5321-928d-4a741bf441a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499738} {:answer/id #uuid "445175a5-5a24-523c-b84c-ed125868ae4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499784} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5b46eb-59d7-5a40-a5bd-d482010a3ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499798} {:answer/id #uuid "5aebe913-e02e-5668-9447-061656dd07be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499829} {:answer/id #uuid "fb14c01b-fcc2-56c9-94a5-1c158a4817f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499798} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6b72617-42bc-5f67-b04d-32f60467b95a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "92c86cbd-ae23-56f6-b895-3b862f7ae3ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-163218" :question/problem "A rectangular plate occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}$\nand has mass density function $\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{1}{9}xy,$ where $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters. The moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $\\frac{13}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nGiven that the center of mass of the plate is at $(2,2),$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}\\cdot \\frac{9}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass $(2,2)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{―}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}((x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3}x(x-2)^{2}y+x{y}^{3}-4x{y}^{2}+4xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\frac{1}{2}x(x-2)^{2}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}-\\frac{4}{3}x{y}^{3}+2x{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{4}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{9}{8}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{8}-18+\\frac{81}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ \\frac{13}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{9}{4}{d}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{9} \\\\ d & =\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "04477c12-4f2b-5395-8bf0-f85ecfa83b11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500416}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "004ce097-91ab-590a-815b-3c51708ab242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499656} {:answer/id #uuid "49e665fd-5611-52a9-b2f7-a3eb3516948a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499795} {:answer/id #uuid "4a855d21-037d-5cfb-851f-7bb85c0b5055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499796} {:answer/id #uuid "62c3f6a0-9789-54d2-8175-2efe5d82c60c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499848} {:answer/id #uuid "a8022c0f-3c93-5f52-9405-a9fafc089d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499656} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1c71d8a-386f-50b9-abbf-82d82b82978a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500245}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a2307504-4cd4-513c-adab-5cca7db9b497" :question/math-academy-id "q-164134" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ with uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{m}$ and is centered at the origin.\nGiven that the moment of inertia of the plate about a line $l$ perpendicular to the plate is $56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2},$ find the distance of the line $l$ from the center of mass of $D.$" :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le \\sqrt{10},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{10})^{2}=10\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{4}{10\\pi }=\\frac{2}{5\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5\\pi }\\cdot 25\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =20\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem and solving for $d,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\ 56 & =20+4{d}^{2} \\\\ 4{d}^{2} & =36 \\\\ {d}^{2} & =9 \\\\ d & =3\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4c0fd999-e432-5b94-beeb-049c81838f59"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129693 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf8414c-f8bd-5f0a-bb90-211754b3e2c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499836} {:answer/id #uuid "c88a091f-5193-58f8-a3ff-ce35311ba8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500063} {:answer/id #uuid "cfb59299-a1c0-5993-8c4a-30e7743496fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500080} {:answer/id #uuid "e625d291-c141-51ba-a082-9a6c4ff3253d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500135} {:answer/id #uuid "eb096d66-a9e1-570b-83d9-620ece8e6bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500135} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f3f5561-87af-512a-8ec3-695976a99517" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "424a10e5-7557-50d9-8260-a7eb80c40d48" :question/math-academy-id "q-38698" :question/problem "Calculate the moment of inertia of a circular plate that occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $1\\,\\text{m}$ with mass density $\\lambda (x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},$ measured in $\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},$ about a line perpendicular to the disk passing through a point on the circumference." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nSince the mass density is symmetric in $x$ and $y,$ its center of mass is at $(0,0).$ The mass density function, in polar coordinates, is given by $\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}=r.$\n\nNext, we find the mass of the plate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{kg}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{r}^{5}{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line passing through a point on the circumference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+{1}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pi }{15}+\\frac{10\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{15}\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4c58d4a3-e259-5a64-bfb1-3db86ff23f95" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500424}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500416 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9200e8ce-dffd-5f07-bf5d-9e83d7aa731d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499938} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9025ee-0539-5c8a-a017-7ac11e31f0d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499990} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f2a651-b2b4-50f6-b49c-4ff309a40cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "33\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500029} {:answer/id #uuid "e119a92f-e3a9-58ff-82e4-e4dd2ae6f760" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500123} {:answer/id #uuid "f4049635-7863-58c6-9d0b-18fbfbee2f14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500156}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500029} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3ca3a19-dcd8-503a-aaa3-09d81f94236f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ea1b0433-500d-513e-b462-b96776148bf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-163813" :question/problem "A circular plate of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region of a disk $D$ of radius $3\\,\\text{m}$ centered at the origin.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $1\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ can be expressed in polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nD=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nThe area of the circular plate $D$ is $A=\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=9\\pi \\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (r,\\theta )=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{6}{9\\pi }=\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid $(0,0).$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}\\lambda (r,\\theta )\\cdot {r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}{|}_{0}^{3}\\cdot \\theta {|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{81}{4}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =27\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $1\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =27+{1}^{2}\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =27+6 \\\\  & =33\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "77423ec6-ca1b-55bf-9ea0-99d7b8c830db" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500403}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500424 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09074932-9828-54d0-9c71-0aa487eef82f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499673} {:answer/id #uuid "127fd677-7aeb-5f9c-8e85-204c2ec5b441" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "58\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499696} {:answer/id #uuid "4274eb44-f29d-5623-a87b-fb74baa6d6ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499780} {:answer/id #uuid "5b88ea4b-a61f-5187-8ed6-13ea9ed676f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "64\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499832} {:answer/id #uuid "e902174a-40ab-5cda-8e58-ebf0d676bfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500137}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499673} :answer-field/id #uuid "d60f4acf-292f-55a9-a007-bfa66a5bd1ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5f04d615-f6b4-5013-9889-65ecf75e2ffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-163173" :question/problem "A rectangular plate of mass $12\\,\\text{kg}$ and uniform mass density occupies the region\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\},$\nwhere $x$ and $y$ are measured in meters.\nFind the moment of inertia of the plate about a line perpendicular to $D$ at a distance of $2\\,\\text{m}$ from a parallel line passing through its center of mass." :question/worked-solution "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe area of the rectangular plate $D$ is $A=2\\cdot 2=4\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.$\nSince the plate has a uniform density, the mass density function is given by\n\n$$\n\\lambda (x,y)=\\frac{M}{A}=\\frac{12}{4}=3\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2},\n$$\n\nand its center of mass is its centroid:\n\n$$\n(\\overset{¯}{x},\\overset{¯}{y})=(\\frac{0+2}{2},\\frac{0+2}{2})=(1,1)\n$$\n\nThen, the moment of inertia about the perpendicular line through the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{M} & ={∬}_{D}((x-\\overset{¯}{x})^{2}+(y-\\overset{¯}{y})^{2})\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3(x-1)^{2}+3(y-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[3(x-1)^{2}y+(y-1)^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}6(x-1)^{2}+2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[2(x-1)^{3}+2x]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2+4-(-2) \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the parallel axis theorem, the moment of inertia about a line $2\\,\\text{m}$ from the center of mass is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I & ={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M \\\\  & =8+{2}^{2}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =8+48 \\\\  & =56\\,\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9445c9d9-c20e-5877-b779-cef068cded52" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500385}}] :activity/title "The Parallel Axis Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499652}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172995} :activity/id #uuid "1249b882-9023-5a8e-b9f3-da0aef8069d3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171895 :question/id #uuid "f6f11512-c37f-5922-b642-5290003b1d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-9492"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "973cf948-eeb3-5c46-b0ac-ee0307a8043a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050779}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259519} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259520} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511492}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "ba2fdaeb-e9c0-578e-a23e-d08f48959fe8" :step/math-academy-id 35542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171896 :question/id #uuid "6753365e-9915-5be9-9a6d-d4c40b2caea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9493"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5777905c-e581-5454-bdbe-29878ad1237b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049303}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275248} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275249} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387395} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447152} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483373} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532715}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Line Integrals of Unity Over Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "a0e6a5fd-e87e-5316-9084-5168c4efa1da" :step/math-academy-id 35543 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172997}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172995 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173201 :tutorial/content "Recall that for a path $C$ in the $xy$-plane, the arc length $L$ of $C$ can be computed as the line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int_C \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is defined parametrically as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle, \\quad a\\leq t \\leq b ,\n$$\n\nthen we can also calculate the arc length of $C$ as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b}||\\mathbf r'(t)||\\,\\textrm d t = \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ we say that the **arc-length element** $\\textrm ds$ for a parametrically defined curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, we can think of $\\textrm ds$ as representing the change in arc length that arises due to a vanishingly small change in $t.$ By summing (integrating) all of these small changes, we arrive at the arc length of $C.$ We'll explore this in more detail at the end of the lesson." :tutorial/id #uuid "56e8bdd3-5303-5eeb-82d3-ef837773c38f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5797 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "65361afd-5d8b-59a0-8bed-041764bc0685" :step/math-academy-id 35544 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172996}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172996 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173202 :tutorial/content "Now suppose that a curve $C$ is defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta), \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nWe've already seen that a polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nUsing the usual formula for the arc length of a polar curve, the arc length of $C$ can be calculated as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as the line integral\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ the arc-length element $\\textrm ds$ for a curve can also be defined using polar coordinates as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll build up a geometric understanding of this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's use this result to calculate some line integrals across polar curves." :tutorial/id #uuid "4850750a-5b8d-5c03-88c1-a2b832a313ce" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5801 :tutorial/title "The Arc Length Element for Polar Curves"} :step/id #uuid "665cda4d-3863-540c-afb3-a1cde7ebd6d9" :step/math-academy-id 35565 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173203 :tutorial/content "Let's now build up a geometric understanding of what the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ means. First, we'll consider the case of a parametric curve defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSuppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b302806eef01fd6e8160ee6e3183959537c0e9f5c6907238a2eea1d208d3da7.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta t$ in the parameter $t.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0b1eb1dcc7be637e4e6024b5a8e9e13a4ea5c3e7439be7a2e3083f5ec274ef71.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = x(t+\\Delta t) - x(t), \\qquad \\Delta y = y(t+\\Delta t) - y(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta y$ are the changes in $x$ and $y,$ respectively, corresponding to the change in $t.$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, these quantities can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\Delta x \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t, \\qquad \\Delta y \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t.\n$$\n\nThe change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ={(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2} \\\\  & ≈{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2} \\\\  & =[{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}](Δt)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s\n\n\\approx\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\Delta t.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta t \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\textrm d t\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "65578a76-05f7-551a-acbd-956ae6831965" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5802 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Parametric Curve"} :step/id #uuid "46feb8c2-5068-5992-a3a2-858b37d8c39e" :step/math-academy-id 35569 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173204 :tutorial/content "Now, let's derive the formula for the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ for a curve defined using plane polar coordinates. We will use a similar geometric approach.\n\nConsider a curve $r= r(\\theta)$ defined using polar coordinates. Suppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/7f/7fa77b8d4ad159967f917b438096f472cbd7f541495847e11b919a6843e6259b.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta \\theta$ in the parameter $\\theta.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/10/105112a2bb993a13282c5f36190ca99a46d80a22a4203f653f7f67060e486d48.png)\n\nFrom here, we see that the vector $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ can be approximated as the vector addition of two components:\n\n- a vector of length $\\Delta r$ acting parallel to $\\mathbf r,$ and\n\n- a vector of length $r\\Delta \\theta$ acting perpendicular to $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/08/080fa1112410328ba369e1c2e5d70fc2e5ddba97606a2fdeaa90d61e3e20d874.png)\n\nNote that $\\Delta r$ is simply the difference of the radial lengths between the points $P$ and $P'.$ The expression $r\\Delta \\theta$ comes from the formula for the arc length of a sector of a circle.\n\nNow, the change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Since our two components are perpendicular, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ≈(Δr)^{2}+(rΔ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =Δ{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta r = r(\\theta + \\Delta\\theta) - r(\\theta) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $(\\Delta s)^2$ above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & ≈{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =[{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}](Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta \\theta \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\textrm d\\theta\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b310dc84-9da7-5492-9639-145086fe7403" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5803 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Arc Length Element for a Polar Curve"} :step/id #uuid "6cd1a226-a48b-5c1c-9eed-9c80bd2de431" :step/math-academy-id 35570}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172176}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483441} :activity/id #uuid "495233f9-f743-506d-b5fc-eaec14701f70" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483441 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{18-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{8-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10-26\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b071591-3de0-5c1f-8aeb-668f34070824" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483473}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483454 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483352 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1c4079-2e4b-58ce-bcdd-cef5dccc4c25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482832} {:answer/id #uuid "2dfd78b7-9d7a-58b9-8cb4-d7bec505415d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\theta {e}^{\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482890} {:answer/id #uuid "33feb924-d253-5720-9471-b530f5057780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482903} {:answer/id #uuid "4170ee29-2690-59a0-ac02-7ef811bc4abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482922} {:answer/id #uuid "42a9d64f-13f1-500c-89f4-8b8e1616488f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\theta }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482925}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482832} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b124e2-32da-5651-8541-39b8419c32ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "22b679e5-bd2f-5f85-b6e8-97dc0feb70a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149799" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\theta {e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=(1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\theta {e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{((1+\\theta ){e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }+(1+\\theta )^{2}{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{e}^{2\\theta }({\\theta }^{2}+1+2\\theta +{\\theta }^{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{e}^{\\theta }\\sqrt{2{\\theta }^{2}+2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3c48a8d6-bb4a-559a-912c-74a8d59f12d8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483472}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483472 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483419 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "614ec109-ddf5-5d69-8b6f-788cffd7d795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} {:answer/id #uuid "654af1d0-1b91-562a-9226-854b3fe81f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482990} {:answer/id #uuid "6bec234f-7e3b-515c-a2ad-33a2c8025357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482999} {:answer/id #uuid "960e21ae-a0b1-56a4-a8e3-7aa555730f7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483072} {:answer/id #uuid "c60010fa-7943-5d78-8597-6d6fec062ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483143}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186482983} :answer-field/id #uuid "87114391-cb4d-5092-83cc-b2e18baed326" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "dcd1fd46-503a-5471-9362-496d88e411cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-149819" :question/problem "![](images/c7/c700a13da4a3b21940ec6268a89670eee850fc6206285bd3b444959a8b386276.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({cos}^{3}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}))}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{6}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3}){cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})[{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})+{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})]}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{4}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{3})\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}[1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{3})]\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\theta +\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\theta }{3})]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "73aaaa88-a71a-530e-8d01-e165dd7e3bfc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483473 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500ad374-633d-5acc-8fc6-2c954b7ceee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482954} {:answer/id #uuid "7daf2602-5ddf-5b93-9c8f-85bf86b1183f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} {:answer/id #uuid "c4195c77-54de-55db-b8bc-7aa9584420e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483138} {:answer/id #uuid "dd638516-5b00-5d2e-b7d7-70996ad3e87f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483185} {:answer/id #uuid "eb789a46-0304-547b-8796-b1b162722c73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483033} :answer-field/id #uuid "416c49b7-a3b7-5531-9507-24f1c4541b91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483261}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6017b841-30bb-5249-81e7-cac41367e88a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123548" :question/problem "![](images/b9/b9ea317575efcb32d8234309e80fecfd4385c99dbe69dd9c33d3fa7d80313bb6.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "748e2a73-740f-5666-bb51-de197b0e3f99" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483506}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483506 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236acfde-42f2-51a3-82ca-3600a09ce0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482876} {:answer/id #uuid "38ff41e9-dd02-5d21-92a8-581369d18b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482910} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac4e6e-fe81-5a84-9636-cabf10c5314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc1c101-f384-5d1e-ab31-e736b726cbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf074d1f-8612-5220-b189-30860f156e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4160743-42ed-5b1d-80ee-6f99ed405134" :question/math-academy-id "q-149786" :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f880bd39-bc57-5d41-b262-cb0554da3861" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483454}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482801}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511645} :activity/id #uuid "2e6736bd-345f-5eb1-b1d1-69bf9d392abd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511593 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259520 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e0accf-355f-56fb-b473-52acb69bd23b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +12{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257002} {:answer/id #uuid "1a282cf9-e726-5893-9709-a1869d24dfa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{17+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257005} {:answer/id #uuid "58bdd781-32a3-5cea-bf8a-eda0cdb4360a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{12+6\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +10{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257551} {:answer/id #uuid "7d2de95a-65cf-5b52-864b-9114a600f1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{12+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +8{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257887} {:answer/id #uuid "d1432cc5-bba9-5179-80ef-98471b08f92d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{4+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +12{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258605}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257005} :answer-field/id #uuid "7adf0afe-c0b4-5de7-8baf-d650968a622b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "77c73302-f62f-524d-b1a1-cbae03250f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-123395" :question/problem "![](images/7d/7dc83cbf45ec3025ad33552ce889c7ecb2daf73b426bc2fd10ec932f3311c92a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{17+8\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta +15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "19f4932e-c75d-5c67-b1d4-b8ff2197751a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bd7e7ed-0cb5-5f2a-9906-20fa4a290dec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256894} {:answer/id #uuid "22a84c4e-ebcd-5545-8a90-d6cd63c6a644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257070} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6fff17-f37b-5bca-8413-fcf9937009c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257313} {:answer/id #uuid "785374b9-120b-5f1f-a6e2-69129997ff6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257847} {:answer/id #uuid "b514f795-53a7-5471-a8c9-56185b112376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256894} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe556346-c85f-5997-afb5-d4236fb40204" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f4d5a329-2172-581a-b274-24fd141bca53" :question/math-academy-id "q-123371" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5b6490495c297b49d394b0f7c494fb35b710c8c4520ab898783877abdd079013.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +16}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{20-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9df4f53a-cfc3-5f5d-9dd3-d0e7ba431674" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511593}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d3de7cb-dc94-55e8-a78d-a27f24ecf883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{4}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510875} {:answer/id #uuid "5df30dae-9f1b-5169-85f4-352ef7b35830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510978} {:answer/id #uuid "8b872d30-3b7e-5aae-91f8-0a201257e3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511071} {:answer/id #uuid "c81c2c1a-18d2-5fae-8d05-046e1ef9709a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{\\theta }}{\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511197} {:answer/id #uuid "f3baf0b1-8376-594a-b0c2-adace7164539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{2\\sqrt{\\theta }}{{\\theta }^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511287}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511071} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ddef5b4-7fd6-526e-90d2-54235e050d54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511337}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4cb23656-32ad-5111-abf0-812956e034d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-149800" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta }$ for $1\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\,?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\frac{1}{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{\\theta })}^{2}+{(-\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\theta }^{2}+1}{{\\theta }^{4}}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\pi }\\frac{\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}+1}}{{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b40a5a22-c678-5bc6-8e83-8d3a7d00ab97"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511546 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01eaff75-9bd9-584b-8dac-798d298e7fe1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510777} {:answer/id #uuid "0d033828-6617-5a30-b0e6-4a3c14d14576" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510806} {:answer/id #uuid "19428a95-6f1a-515e-aa64-ad5b042f4aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\pi /4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510840} {:answer/id #uuid "51635b1e-3cd9-5e5e-8da8-a31c41a6b5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510956} {:answer/id #uuid "e233503b-2829-57e2-afff-b160c60d6d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511251}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510956} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2bc4d54-53d7-5960-8596-76e1189178c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b688c283-ffe1-5279-a25b-e224f4238243" :question/math-academy-id "q-123634" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{2}{e}^{\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{2\\theta }+2{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}2{e}^{\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\theta }{|}_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /2}-{e}^{\\pi /4}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi /4}({e}^{\\pi /4}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ca26c44a-47b3-5082-aab1-eac96843df54" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511675}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511675 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511541 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "051f2eaa-30f6-5a12-9a77-9f95ac0b3e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510788} {:answer/id #uuid "3257bf5c-fa92-5d61-948f-9e8f3c674e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510889} {:answer/id #uuid "5896cb3f-bb8d-57b4-adca-c7ec3f6be4d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510970} {:answer/id #uuid "bda1540c-a5f2-5558-ad30-970a0713c6a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511174} {:answer/id #uuid "dba7e57b-8972-5648-baf2-34e857e37757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511235}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510788} :answer-field/id #uuid "99e9e8ce-81b5-5865-823b-16944100d9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "acde0039-9e1f-5668-bb5b-a5df57c98611" :question/math-academy-id "q-123643" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta })^{2}+{(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )^{2}+{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,\\theta {|}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\text{ }(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "e9a0c7e4-f9f1-5b84-a2be-688ec5da197a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511657}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510756}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532756} :activity/id #uuid "822bcaa0-c52b-52f8-b781-cc9986a54dc7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532733 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447154 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17652adb-f0bd-5e06-b55e-fb60b41d8171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447094} {:answer/id #uuid "392cdecf-382d-5eaf-8512-13e93f090b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447104} {:answer/id #uuid "bb292c1a-79be-51a1-91b7-2783d7d709cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447133} {:answer/id #uuid "e84048f5-bc2b-5cb4-a79e-ebf71a21e3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447139} {:answer/id #uuid "fdef13fa-c9e2-5073-8dc0-04da2e76ab8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447140}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447104} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cca78f2-2d1d-5264-9180-c7409ad32bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "586f6a61-ad62-56e5-9a2c-611ea0a2819c" :question/math-academy-id "q-123379" :question/problem "![](images/19/19725cfd8068973c899fcb4f54abcebbbfcd24bd68aec510b9d41cee2c2cd02a.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(2-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +16{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +16}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{20-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1b608b4b-9e61-5390-845a-6b9f724dc703" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07e65c36-be44-5f01-9c76-e2d474ae0429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{16+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482823} {:answer/id #uuid "416c60e9-e558-531b-9580-88325c39f159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{18-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482921} {:answer/id #uuid "d1393249-3bf9-5edc-8048-09a57dbf1249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{8-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483160} {:answer/id #uuid "d1fcdafd-bf7d-5de0-8c88-50a987f3891f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} {:answer/id #uuid "e65466c4-bccb-5f49-930c-7f3a5aefd1ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{10-26\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483163} :answer-field/id #uuid "86b07591-e098-5b5d-80e3-d30542d3cd68" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "49e7f17d-b251-56fb-a73b-9aa663a5feb5" :question/math-academy-id "q-123410" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef5fe44009ea42457243052cff584a216ee94636d8e3c16ff9446e85b8eca551.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{(1-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +25{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{1-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +25}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{7\\pi /6}\\sqrt{26-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "87995df5-9cb4-59d7-9813-d9c7cc1db2d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa492a8-e07a-522a-bf45-34af18c7a267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{2} θ\\tan^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168598} {:answer/id #uuid "1813a2d0-d5bd-52b4-99b5-efcb1d2a7640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168599} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba48aa-c769-5c6a-9e07-f0c67be25a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + 9\\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168600} {:answer/id #uuid "55f60188-6938-5f92-aae8-dedef3328cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{9 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a3ab0-69fc-59cc-bc73-08e310482454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168602}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4363fdf-f405-59b8-b8f3-f8bb7ba8806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab8694-5f19-5ea3-8d98-092aec26cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405025} {:answer/id #uuid "a57cacb1-b76d-579c-8c67-b4527f5232ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{9+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405026} {:answer/id #uuid "1eabb9a5-ecc0-5827-9b8a-fa684cf58678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405027} {:answer/id #uuid "9f675dbc-4798-5a7f-b170-06e1c7c60f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405028}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9492d74f-ea7c-56c6-a9a7-679ef961d276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is obtained by using the pointwise polar arc-length integrand for ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2<\\theta<\\pi /2$? Interpret the endpoint integrals as improper integrals." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese endpoint integrals are improper and diverge to $+\\infty.$ The requested expression is determined by the pointwise arc-length integrand on $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2.$"} :step/id #uuid "cd77c6be-b59a-50ba-9fc2-79d740821700" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532756 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dd37ec-93a9-5b21-89e4-561d7cf50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8e4a81-ce37-5a0a-bf71-1d9989cbe036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256881} {:answer/id #uuid "297e7d94-4a80-565f-88bd-e5215df2ba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257151} {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ca18d-520e-5091-a63b-d25c9509aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b3878a-36da-5c94-8d4e-f1633afbca83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c8bb8d5-c684-5d53-8ebb-fc2e85b6f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123355" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d5675ffb-cacc-5ee5-aa57-add6376682b0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532733}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532715 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de248ef-eae0-53e3-95b5-fbeb5926dd60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532063} {:answer/id #uuid "61bbc39f-0989-5704-9418-422cadcf22ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532151} {:answer/id #uuid "91cf89e0-afa8-591c-bc93-ee02e4978fb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4-2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532265} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3eced4-b5ba-5283-97ad-820c6e28c0ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532339} {:answer/id #uuid "cbde0e1b-dda5-5de9-b0d5-ad1180eecbdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532372}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532372} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d67ad9-bea8-55c4-a76f-57659a7c98ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532577}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef442bea-bb64-52fe-bd5a-d969e4e9ca72" :question/math-academy-id "q-123558" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c27ab9b0062fabfe1fdbfd43631bf1f143c0fa4ec80587519e9ce6c4e84e7b2b.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}-\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}-0) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f7f86389-8351-5a11-bac8-76649d974f77"}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531934}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543661} :activity/id #uuid "1f3a6185-41af-5ad8-935e-01846db70555" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94106bf7-442c-57b7-a8d4-7d3725cd3301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f17b3f-b619-5663-a742-8cd16caad422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363373} {:answer/id #uuid "2f268941-c5e1-5f87-9aff-31416118fe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+8{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363374} {:answer/id #uuid "63cf36ad-cc0f-5781-9b02-c40c452e845e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+12{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363375} {:answer/id #uuid "eac417bd-f5e6-53b0-85c1-fea6d7f50ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+40{sin}^{2}⁡2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363372} :answer-field/id #uuid "388c2572-dc24-57d0-9cb1-bc6bf8263984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cd418bfc-f7c5-506e-bc72-5623879aa6a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-149798" :question/problem "![](images/c6/c6556825089b3b475615ed4a8ebf9eff2e6db3d76eb26103312f736a3e114793.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta $ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{8},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}+{(-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +64{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta +60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{4+60{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /8}\\sqrt{1+15{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a4bc5a2d-3e93-51e5-9f99-7400e1837432"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "236acfde-42f2-51a3-82ca-3600a09ce0a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482876} {:answer/id #uuid "38ff41e9-dd02-5d21-92a8-581369d18b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186482910} {:answer/id #uuid "8eac4e6e-fe81-5a84-9636-cabf10c5314f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} {:answer/id #uuid "acc1c101-f384-5d1e-ab31-e736b726cbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483104} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c5c96c-0c63-5cb2-88af-e9d02ac33333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483115}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186483061} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf074d1f-8612-5220-b189-30860f156e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186483319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4160743-42ed-5b1d-80ee-6f99ed405134" :question/math-academy-id "q-149786" :question/problem "![](images/24/24f7ae31455abc10e60814b6b599966c92680422432c82f6cc7386d6d414f29c.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ae34bece-dd6d-55aa-a39e-6fffe351a9e0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0081acc5-b8a0-5432-9f86-16b58fe1f445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{8+10\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +10\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447091} {:answer/id #uuid "1015c73e-191b-5d23-bc1d-08512f03b481" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+6{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta +8\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447093} {:answer/id #uuid "369565c3-561a-50ca-9be4-d934f4abc68d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +6\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447100} {:answer/id #uuid "609847c3-196c-5c58-9386-82fb40230109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +8{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447111} {:answer/id #uuid "8a907a7b-6b1a-50eb-9d99-ac2cfde55650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447117}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447111} :answer-field/id #uuid "970ad32f-3bc8-5eae-a83f-06f38e693fb2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c80223d-79b2-5d97-8d88-3fa6275a012a" :question/math-academy-id "q-123377" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3b8ef36c29d1babc5c3ccf1d474678a18ed5937b883e026d18d5c7ea1aaaa3f5.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(3+\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta )^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡3\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{9+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +1+8{cos}^{2}⁡3\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{10+6\\operatorname{sin}⁡3\\theta +8{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c0a58790-b2fc-5728-aa3b-02bd91182c99" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543670}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543670 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405029 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac3d5894-f4c5-5d9e-ab7c-cd4cec7b9228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{3+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405031} {:answer/id #uuid "098ef428-487b-52de-8bb3-157513d5af5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405032} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec24e38-78a7-59a2-bcbd-116c50a31d93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405033} {:answer/id #uuid "2fff1e42-09de-569f-8b26-6742481f41f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405034} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a7a56e-be07-55b4-a841-3621b9f27de7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405035}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186405034} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aaf3974-45a7-5d36-b332-ebb301287f47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9debd71f-7b2d-5a32-ae10-ca946736e9d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123458" :question/problem "Which of the following gives ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s$ with the arc-length integrand simplified pointwise in the given polar angle $\\theta$ (on each smooth piece, without reparametrizing), where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi $ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhere $1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta>0$, $r=\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nApplying the arc-length formula piecewise between the finitely many zeros of $r$, the expression for our line integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta })^{2}+{(\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡4\\theta }{\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )+\\frac{4{cos}^{2}⁡4\\theta }{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1-{sin}^{2}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )^{2}+4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta )[1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta +4-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta ]}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡4\\theta }\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ea17984d-b1fd-5109-a5c3-c2c1166176dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ec25039-c0fd-56ca-b984-bd9349c79ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta /2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363365} {:answer/id #uuid "93316eec-08b8-5888-a518-1f7f028efff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} {:answer/id #uuid "ddd3e2e0-b449-5ae9-bfe5-39ae00b8ab32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363367} {:answer/id #uuid "827145a1-4259-51ff-8504-aef48c2e2ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363368} {:answer/id #uuid "0a472974-80e2-5c15-a84b-22f9c2f5a79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363369}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363366} :answer-field/id #uuid "5703c778-a247-5102-af88-f5464e7312d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "929c6f43-55a6-50e3-98c8-0627bab8c6ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-149788" :question/problem "![](images/03/03cacb8c2da76ff806a3f8a41cf18d3b57f67eeb60d6efa0cdfbfce770496c60.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{-\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-{e}^{-\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{-\\theta })}^{2}+{(-{e}^{\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{-2\\theta }+{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{-2\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{-\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "f760da62-3000-5957-aaa0-e2654d0801a1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186543651}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542615}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046669} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553606} :activity/id #uuid "380fb825-5be1-580a-990c-bf11f8d48700" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049041 :topic/id #uuid "d56dd46d-c76b-5160-925e-c4ddd2d90833" :topic/math-academy-id 3354} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f429c0-4106-53ea-9c7e-5a3e5c69557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} {:answer/id #uuid "0560e91f-841f-5133-b19f-414819f58310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}(2{e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362985} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c2c04d-6401-5b56-9894-aeead7dbe986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362986} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc5fcaa-258a-5aeb-90ce-1bd2af6582b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362987} {:answer/id #uuid "a210ea76-975d-59a6-8399-df6d3b035510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b62b53e-864d-5c41-bf41-a59840c727e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f2b06f7c-408a-5dfe-b281-0fde1f80e146" :question/math-academy-id "q-123354" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d33f9b851125443ff738fa4c3aedcbeaf8a202d05caa06747e74262c0ae89bd9.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20042ad6-2b50-5151-ad49-bb4eef91cd09" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553632}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259536 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336fa244-06f6-591f-8966-fdfd73c6a423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{(\\pi +2)}^{1/2}-8}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257235} {:answer/id #uuid "58895e89-7919-506e-994d-1c2dbe196d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{({\\pi }^{2}+4)}^{3/2}-8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257550} {:answer/id #uuid "c128e791-18c2-5301-8880-f7b4f01bfcb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{({\\pi }^{2}+4)}^{3/2}-4}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258470} {:answer/id #uuid "e274d3fe-784b-5699-8844-849e5934ed3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{(\\pi +2)}^{3/2}-8}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258753} {:answer/id #uuid "f808119a-e030-585c-82c2-c558a63e63a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{(\\pi +4)}^{1/2}-8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258958}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257550} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4736e82-40af-567d-b32a-974a616d81a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259346}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f5451ce0-d618-51ea-8499-28c757a17f08" :question/math-academy-id "q-123587" :question/problem "![](images/99/994b8cd2134d6d1e9a1e89559e462128c05fd5d3e7f4755c51931f3cf7f37268.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={\\theta }^{2}\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={\\theta }^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{({\\theta }^{2})}^{2}+{(2\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{4}+4{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{\\theta }^{2}(4+{\\theta }^{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\theta \\sqrt{4+{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\theta \\sqrt{4+{\\theta }^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\frac{{(4+{\\theta }^{2})}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{{({\\pi }^{2}+4)}^{3/2}-8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "22e62c73-21df-5fda-9d79-ba9cdeb1a7a9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553635}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553632 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400107 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04289a9d-17cf-573a-9701-d41c64ef2496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}(5+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400109} {:answer/id #uuid "2cd6606e-b516-5f6f-9f4b-c6f100ea81db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400110} {:answer/id #uuid "41205926-9dd0-53e7-b1aa-ade02572e0a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{3+5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400111} {:answer/id #uuid "be2d7cad-36f0-55ab-b08f-a68a53ffb570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400112} {:answer/id #uuid "4933aa14-2d33-5b3a-973b-f6175f55c3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400113}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400112} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bff7a0b-d96e-5598-95de-cbbd3ac2a012" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400108}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ffdfd696-312e-58bb-aa35-858898cb0c00" :question/math-academy-id "q-123519" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,$ for $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta })}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )+\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{4(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )(4-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "76362486-adbf-5459-b248-d212c1251f2c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553635 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2dc039c6-5665-5166-aa53-9a15c6c4c91b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272795} {:answer/id #uuid "8b464717-8539-5469-8060-dac626a0833e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273588} {:answer/id #uuid "d4ea6766-0861-5a8d-8643-ea7eabf5bda5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274253} {:answer/id #uuid "d920ab5e-c0b5-5fd4-a193-1324a5a2e7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274284} {:answer/id #uuid "e41b2b91-8816-5099-b2fa-6f471a4a0905" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274403}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272795} :answer-field/id #uuid "49db0e6a-b203-52c9-99a3-d2fa536b85d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4554a25e-9962-5d01-9655-91afa5f0b772" :question/math-academy-id "q-149815" :question/problem "![](images/62/624bb949d80c1da50cba951b00210a2a26aeb3c44d77d4c2eed69c8e3ba0f4d4.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7d0d777b-efed-57ba-b08b-6f64a58115f0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447160 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2aeb4a-6148-5344-8241-a30ca0414bf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+5{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447092} {:answer/id #uuid "369bbef4-e31e-5ef6-9afb-de7d3e21d28f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447101} {:answer/id #uuid "39da32fb-4fd3-56f4-8b48-27bfa06dac2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+8{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447105} {:answer/id #uuid "7d101eaf-b053-5e48-a386-fa7d17ca7d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{5+4{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447114} {:answer/id #uuid "ba9bc000-67c4-53d2-b325-fa53d6ea89eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+7{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447132}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447101} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8dcb75d-dac3-5c81-8f6e-1e4763779208" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fb00dc0e-66fe-5382-b23d-00ac1fa661b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-123559" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\pi /3\\le \\theta \\le \\pi /2?$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-3{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({csc}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-3{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{csc}^{6}⁡\\theta {cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{csc}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi /2}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "952b8495-fb82-537a-8ec9-22ad3d4e7fad" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553607}}] :activity/title "Further Properties of Line Integrals of Scalar Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552854}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577} :activity/id #uuid "37fac92b-9502-52a2-8069-399fccbd8a62" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048707}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561381} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167331} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284666} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383607} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561337} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561373}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"} :step/id #uuid "a6ab275a-61df-5648-811a-176fda344fe5" :step/math-academy-id 30861 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526461} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167332} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360570} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526426} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526441} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526442} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526455}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"} :step/id #uuid "8627eaba-93e3-51d5-b350-72ee5af3e635" :step/math-academy-id 30862 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529504} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284669} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360483} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360484} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378038} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529488} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186529494}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "60b2ea1e-4b9a-5557-99ae-a83016e785a6" :step/math-academy-id 30863 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166588 :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546131} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167329} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360510} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360511} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546100} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186546118}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "26ddca41-ae16-5395-ae11-41c4d70303c6" :step/math-academy-id 30864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168061 :tutorial/content "The chain rule for single variable functions $f=f(x)$ and $x=x(t)$ states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}.\n$$\n\nLet's now suppose that $f = f(x,y),$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t)$ are single-variable functions. How do we find $\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d t}$ in this case?\n\nThe functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ are known as **intermediate variables.** They are independent variables for the function $f(x,y),$ but are themselves dependent on the variable $t.$\n\nOne way to consider how $f(x,y)$ depends on $t$ is to draw a tree diagram.\n\nStarting with $f(x,y),$ we draw a branch for each intermediate variable. Then, we draw a branch for both $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ which are both dependent on the variable $t.$\n\n![](images/cd/cdc47e038ecaba62ae612a0ac0bc07067af232353fc4fc169bf4be57b3abbd1f.png)\n\nWe then label each branch with the derivative describing how the function changes due to a change in the variable. \n\n![](images/ec/ece6012e358b5b6920395df88acd8d7037a7b15bed1add1220365ba72bd24cbd.png)\n\nThen, traversing each branch, we compute the product of the derivatives along each branch and place the result at the end of the branch.\n\n![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nFinally, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we sum the results for each branch:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}\n = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nThis result is called the **multivariable chain rule**." :tutorial/id #uuid "80ad8a26-7d43-52d6-b0ba-e1d04283e5ad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4957 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "03fe551f-81c6-5c42-9baf-d6d12895ab47" :step/math-academy-id 30893 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167573}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168062 :tutorial/content "How do we apply the multivariable chain rule if the intermediate variables are also dependent on two variables? \n\nFor example, suppose we have\n\n$$\nf = f(x,y), \\qquad x=g(s,t), \\qquad y=h(s,t).\n$$\n\nHow do we compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}?$ \n\nFirst, let's construct a tree diagram.\n\n![](images/ca/ca524eda2e6be3354fcb72bed52fcaf22257073c3228d74850f8226f9ef0af6f.png)\n\nNow, since we're interested in $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s},$ we highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $s:$\n\n![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nIf we keep $t$ fixed and differentiate $f$ with respect to $s,$ then according to the chain rule for two intermediate variables and one independent variable, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial s} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if we wanted to compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t},$ we would highlight all branches that give differentiation with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nKeeping $s$ fixed and differentiating $f$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "58d189b1-31fb-5b1b-bc4c-cff6be5e2f7b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4958 :tutorial/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Two Independent Variables"} :step/id #uuid "2ffafece-1c0b-50f7-a3b1-e1b2d1bada36" :step/math-academy-id 30894 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168063 :tutorial/content "We can generalize the chain rule to a multivariable function containing an arbitrary number of intermediate and independent variables.\n\nConsider the multivariable function \n\n$$\nf = f(x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_m)\n$$\n\nwith $m$ independent variables and, for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots, n\\},$ the variable \n\n$$\nx_i = g_i(t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_n)\n$$\n\nis a differentiable function of $n$ independent variables. Then,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t_j} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_1}{\\partial t_j} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_2}{\\partial t_j} + \\dots + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_m} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x_m}{\\partial t_j}\n$$\n\nfor $j \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots, n\\}.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1ba7d2a4-05a6-5e62-8035-2610239633c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4959 :tutorial/title "The Generalized Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "9731a04f-c2d1-5342-b940-0313b71ca1eb" :step/math-academy-id 30895 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167697 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168088 :tutorial/content "There is a special case of the multivariable chain rule when $f=f(x,y)$ and $y=g(x).$ \n\nNotice that $x$ is an independent variable here. Therefore, the chain rule becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot {{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}x}}_{⏟}}_{1}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44effd03-02e7-5eb1-8732-ee1522afa147" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5184 :tutorial/title "A Special Case of the Multivariable Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "fdc61eff-9eab-552e-9092-f4040bdff21a" :step/math-academy-id 32539 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167574}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186166978}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420998} :activity/id #uuid "844676a2-0a90-5f03-a7c1-22515b371e49" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a19a158-cb30-5c9e-9cf9-891ab114ad36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(2t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355012} {:answer/id #uuid "392f1f1c-1dad-5e72-b1e4-40cf201a767c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}t(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355179} {:answer/id #uuid "869f5f73-0c65-5003-ade6-8eeef3e8b4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355855} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f0fe58-16c4-5d79-ab11-4893e9ff089a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(s-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356369} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ace28-41b6-5a2e-8b64-9dee698d22fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314b903-87e4-5c8c-a8db-703d520d008b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1e3cf4a-155f-587d-ba03-cc737759407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94448" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y,$ where $x=2st$ and $y={s}^{2}t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2st)=4st, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(4st)(2t)+(-4)(2st) \\\\  & =8s{t}^{2}-8st \\\\  & =8st(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2cf7698e-82a3-5979-93cb-d2edffbb99d7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9a8991-24fd-50f9-8941-a329e3133c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+10{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321230} {:answer/id #uuid "84b790fb-dce2-5ea5-9fbd-fb66967b78c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-20s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322195} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f027a-f67b-5d1f-b407-9ee3b85b3667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322598} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bfc905-fde6-54a5-83ad-c5fa1e325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} {:answer/id #uuid "f675ee7a-8c5d-5a9a-90cb-d0e4dd971fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-15s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1de9ae-38d9-5d66-a275-9fc76c319cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bd5b5db6-0961-5b1c-8f50-24c2f4348bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37132" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-5yz,$ where $x=st,$ $y={s}^{3}$ and $z=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-5z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)({s}^{3})=2{s}^{4}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(st)^{2}-5(s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-5{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2{s}^{4}t)\\cdot t+({s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3{s}^{2}+(-5{s}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-15{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a9db776-142d-5ace-9b62-834d4e4b6a63" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421001}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "021a846a-8825-55bf-a7b3-1dc5c5c5ff19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281709} {:answer/id #uuid "49b2d0a3-8640-5928-a7d2-f8a14102a68f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a1e1c7-9e8a-5f17-9500-4d7507d44b3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283715} {:answer/id #uuid "efd819c9-60d2-5826-9266-3dba4a49aaec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283779} {:answer/id #uuid "f5de75cd-c44e-5baf-9f07-d98f3333edab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186282314} :answer-field/id #uuid "d658fcd0-7d37-5a87-a5b6-42706ffef583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cdba7da9-8893-51f6-a38d-eccc847b176c" :question/math-academy-id "q-170670" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)$ and $y={x}^{2}+1,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+1},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2x({x}^{2}+1)}{{x}^{2}+1}=2x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6ef0c797-03b2-58eb-8a37-83236f476fe8"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be18679-efe0-59e6-80ac-db92f8d96a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9edd72-2523-57c6-9665-681f1ed475c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358439} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcdf56c-f98e-5632-8b37-69db930425d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b3c97-4572-526c-8afd-d0a1d5757af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359141} {:answer/id #uuid "b33efd16-e5a4-5396-8988-6df4afae8ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f643ac2-8342-50cb-9136-e026a1d69059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfb1f74a-0323-5d9e-a2f6-877356ed98f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95118" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+yx$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7bfbd495-1860-54a3-8d60-35f838cfca25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421001 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420996 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17573a89-2ada-5cfd-8592-1b89b18d61f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420990} {:answer/id #uuid "3856cad9-d2c1-55a4-adf6-784ae35a6aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420991} {:answer/id #uuid "5c54b913-d15a-53bc-b2c6-f71477530f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420992} {:answer/id #uuid "ae277bb1-236f-56f1-8a0c-7fd47295108c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} {:answer/id #uuid "d87585d5-b5cd-5fbc-b58c-a3d2c45f4e66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420993} :answer-field/id #uuid "f118ee24-10a3-5bda-8238-4be0365cace0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f22c7a35-af74-59c8-b71b-cae8347ea97d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37206" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+{z}^{2},$ where $x={s}^{2}+{t}^{2},$ $y=\\frac{s}{t}$ and $z=s{t}^{2}.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+{c}_{1}t+{c}_{2}st+{c}_{3}s{t}^{4},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x+z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=y+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{s}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{1}{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{s}{t})\\cdot 2s+({s}^{2}+{t}^{2}+s{t}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(\\frac{s}{t}+2s{t}^{2})\\cdot {t}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{s}^{2}}{t}+\\frac{{s}^{2}}{t}+t+st+st+2s{t}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{s}^{2}}{t}+t+2st+2s{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=1+2+2=5.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "926bef20-92d0-5cd4-928e-9a8dd40763b1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186420997}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420989}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515709} :activity/id #uuid "f9135b23-a1e2-5324-b00b-b9b69f18dd59" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515621 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01632887-adf0-501e-9c55-0c43a8e3a195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357770} {:answer/id #uuid "0667969c-90fd-5446-9ffe-efe916cf6782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357809} {:answer/id #uuid "31a57553-0726-5177-afb7-c33ab426fd0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358192} {:answer/id #uuid "c24d7296-dc42-53b5-9df3-6afb6f82d279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359456} {:answer/id #uuid "ed7d37b8-0f46-5fbb-b1ee-35edde54b6d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359864}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357809} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae361e8-131f-5a9f-a75b-7ea0d6de8cc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "878a1123-ef9a-5a02-91a8-3dc4077906e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-94749" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=xy,$ where $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st).$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st), \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))(s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(st))+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st))(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s{cos}^{2}⁡(st)-s{sin}^{2}⁡(st) \\\\  & =s({cos}^{2}⁡(st)-{sin}^{2}⁡(st)) \\\\  & =s\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2st).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03f2df0e-7127-5bf6-a3cb-55f1736639ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d5ecadd-1d99-51f3-b3ee-0d0a52a7e504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478669} {:answer/id #uuid "7eececa3-aed5-56bc-8acd-83a7d9f52ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{1+2{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478748} {:answer/id #uuid "c04864f4-8e0e-5d9d-8763-0a9d20cfc1c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478870} {:answer/id #uuid "c22286df-0bd2-569b-80cb-dd78460f6e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478871} {:answer/id #uuid "fd87021b-423b-576b-b08a-47b84980a27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478956}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478956} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f1b30ab-e87a-541b-926a-088f06d1391d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "889d0bfa-8294-56c7-a3ce-0b0d572aff69" :question/math-academy-id "q-95120" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={e}^{2x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}\\cdot 2{e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+{e}^{2x}}+\\frac{2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+2{e}^{2x}}{x+{e}^{2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3c096bca-7b75-5ffa-8b01-bdd0cb2ca5b9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515674}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515674 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383614 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8172957c-8ded-58d3-9a23-800391f6abc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383616} {:answer/id #uuid "65fc28e8-01f1-5779-81a2-95006d854621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{s}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383617} {:answer/id #uuid "78f76e21-a29b-5ac4-bef0-5e96cc36b301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{s}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383618} {:answer/id #uuid "1650585c-4f5b-5c09-98dc-1b8f36155d98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{s}+2+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383619} {:answer/id #uuid "9d243851-27bb-5ff4-9d66-8de5b61c5110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{e}^{s}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383620}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383618} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31244f4-c1a2-50a4-adcb-bc5510fae670" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186383615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "04777d40-e9e1-5da3-94ee-34ffd4874f77" :question/math-academy-id "q-170674" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=z-x-y,$ where $x={e}^{s}+t,$ $y=s+1$ and $z=1-t-s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(-1)\\cdot {e}^{s}+(-1)\\cdot 1+(1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-{e}^{s}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a1ec6cd0-639d-5c32-a34b-e1f726d9e5e0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515687 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167325 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a19a158-cb30-5c9e-9cf9-891ab114ad36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(2t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355012} {:answer/id #uuid "392f1f1c-1dad-5e72-b1e4-40cf201a767c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}t(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355179} {:answer/id #uuid "869f5f73-0c65-5003-ade6-8eeef3e8b4ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{s}^{2}(s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355855} {:answer/id #uuid "c2f0fe58-16c4-5d79-ab11-4893e9ff089a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(s-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356369} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ace28-41b6-5a2e-8b64-9dee698d22fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8st(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186356853} :answer-field/id #uuid "6314b903-87e4-5c8c-a8db-703d520d008b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e1e3cf4a-155f-587d-ba03-cc737759407e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94448" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y,$ where $x=2st$ and $y={s}^{2}t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2st)=4st, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(4st)(2t)+(-4)(2st) \\\\  & =8s{t}^{2}-8st \\\\  & =8st(t-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bac60d39-22a6-5ac6-a280-51042791f62d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515621}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515709 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385370 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "894ca471-2dd4-5d40-b36a-8da27b160d4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385372} {:answer/id #uuid "a0652b0f-d1a7-5fb6-a24a-41d904eaf606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385373} {:answer/id #uuid "290a2a7e-ad92-516f-ae90-67ba3132c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385374} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad83d4e-e307-5e90-9e4f-cc60c72f71d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385375} {:answer/id #uuid "10e3eea0-e64b-5459-8cc2-03bcb5ecde36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385376}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385375} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ca2715-a0aa-5460-b75b-4a47465638ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "7b9407c6-12a8-5179-a6fb-edc61bbc79bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-37208" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{y}^{2}z,$ where $x=3t,$ $y=t-s$ and $z=2st.$ Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+{c}_{1}s{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{s}^{2}t+{c}_{3}{s}^{3},\n$$\n\nfind the value of ${c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(t-s)(2st)=4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =(t-s)^{2}={t}^{2}-2st+{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =3, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t)\\cdot 3+(4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t)\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}-2st+{s}^{2})\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+4s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t+2s{t}^{2}-4{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{3} \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡3t+6s{t}^{2}-8{s}^{2}t+2{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=6,$ ${c}_{2}=-8$ and ${c}_{3}=2,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=6+(-8)+2=0.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe99fc10-51d6-55ce-8bf8-4a3e58f9b8d2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515687}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514869}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519644} :activity/id #uuid "c22e0dcd-15c2-55db-9d25-3bdc2b452a55" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519617 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167331 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aa7cb58-130b-52e2-9ffc-cc9e52330663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358818} {:answer/id #uuid "a1a9cb0c-c43b-5a83-8c67-e7c35f7eae70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359161} {:answer/id #uuid "ea13c98f-b638-5d97-8167-fa566662cb6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t-\\operatorname{cot}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359835} {:answer/id #uuid "ec49dc46-0ba5-5fda-b9c2-6bec956ac528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359856} {:answer/id #uuid "f7162188-93f3-50e1-8684-87bec1a6cf00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359856} :answer-field/id #uuid "a955c8c5-959c-5957-86ce-5b5f4f04ba90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "19a64e51-db17-58fb-852b-340494362842" :question/math-academy-id "q-95078" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2}),x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}$ for $0<t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d1473e6-3651-50ed-9ec9-88f004e468cd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519619 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360556 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b80e388-89f0-55d3-9647-29eb1e7d21a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357986} {:answer/id #uuid "2931a144-34bf-5b91-9fd0-2c8561636d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s+{csc}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358129} {:answer/id #uuid "5f982da7-fadd-5a18-83b3-b5af485d42fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358586} {:answer/id #uuid "981660b8-6d8e-5d6b-a353-cc9e60ac0aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359084} {:answer/id #uuid "d2b01e60-3c8a-5b80-ab36-ad8a68a5308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{sec}^{2}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359593}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357986} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce0a50c8-6fe1-52e2-992d-16da1ca6ce29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f22cdf5c-424c-5c09-91c4-a4b6bd7748c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-95084" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y},x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s)+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s} \\\\  & =-2-2{cot}^{2}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1f2d63e2-db91-599f-ba79-b154350da4c1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519639}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247ee7d0-904c-5d01-8297-06a98015037c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{s}}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385840} {:answer/id #uuid "7cec0dc1-33bb-5f04-a034-05527df9b19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+s){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385841} {:answer/id #uuid "323b91bd-668a-507d-8f83-8a04b4f2b115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(2+\\sqrt{s}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385842} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd6bb8a-7411-5765-b787-90052e6b40f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385843} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbb05f2-b967-534b-9feb-494c22d63500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385844}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385844} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f70704d-2718-514e-b670-1b5ed75416ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c583feca-7024-59b3-b46e-988493997541" :question/math-academy-id "q-95083" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ $x=\\sqrt{s}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2d471feb-727e-5216-935f-6bc8969b2a48" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519619}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519639 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167332 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e272a6d-78d3-5cb9-95c9-c87f0fea3da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357869} {:answer/id #uuid "34692782-e7a3-51e2-939e-c7595c3e9b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358219} {:answer/id #uuid "69e98fd7-fe36-540b-bd06-de9d5d7bdda7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+{x}^{2})-\\frac{1+3{x}^{2}}{1+2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358677} {:answer/id #uuid "7b57f18a-b391-5d2a-a9af-d8d06a8a7f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)-\\frac{1+2{x}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358823} {:answer/id #uuid "9695921f-e190-55c7-a45b-cceb0f8f0066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357869} :answer-field/id #uuid "000855cc-b4eb-5315-a2d3-71276d48abd0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "56630566-56de-5b10-a1f2-1989c1203d78" :question/math-academy-id "q-95121" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x+2{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x(1+2x)}{x(1+x)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6bc8b7a0-5c37-5a1c-8102-88692aa5f318"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1830bf59-5084-598f-b1f6-81191dfbfbcf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2+t}{\\sqrt{t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357965} {:answer/id #uuid "26a1cc25-ba10-5462-a7f5-4b6c422818d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358100} {:answer/id #uuid "72ac5140-e75a-5b93-834e-7afad4225743" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358751} {:answer/id #uuid "8cdd033a-0457-5b60-bc47-43fcb08320c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{t}}{\\sqrt{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358978} {:answer/id #uuid "8ddb28d7-a49c-5dad-b528-bbf182a53bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358988}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358988} :answer-field/id #uuid "146b2117-94cd-5963-92fa-afe7f73a78f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e0f22ddd-635a-5638-92e4-fbad8751067f" :question/math-academy-id "q-95077" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2xy},x=t$ and $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2xy}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2xy}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}\\cdot 1+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{\\sqrt{2t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "773b2d36-2f0a-57e0-8be2-e48bb09a4815" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519617}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518986}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524044} :activity/id #uuid "55cff9f9-18c9-533a-bc34-1923b1ce73b6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050031 :topic/id #uuid "27615ef6-ec0e-5e91-b58b-24b70dc01a09" :topic/math-academy-id 3173} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360569 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4be18679-efe0-59e6-80ac-db92f8d96a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9edd72-2523-57c6-9665-681f1ed475c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358439} {:answer/id #uuid "9bcdf56c-f98e-5632-8b37-69db930425d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359116} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b3c97-4572-526c-8afd-d0a1d5757af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359141} {:answer/id #uuid "b33efd16-e5a4-5396-8988-6df4afae8ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359315}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358407} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f643ac2-8342-50cb-9136-e026a1d69059" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cfb1f74a-0323-5d9e-a2f6-877356ed98f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-95118" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+yx$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "03875695-6306-58ee-ade0-e90c339315ac" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524107}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0906d5df-95ee-50f2-a42b-09e69555e339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4t(1+2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357833} {:answer/id #uuid "140ea72d-9a0d-57b5-b67c-2049bb317343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357925} {:answer/id #uuid "9cea513d-612f-5290-b731-384d792ca237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359124} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ba0846-4e92-5a2d-b302-7d756aa55faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{t}^{3}(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359440} {:answer/id #uuid "e95d1d3f-a370-59d0-97ce-9062ff9fd3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4t(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359826}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359124} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9bfd21b-4c44-5c5c-ba56-5fadb4ae0273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4d8c80e5-aa7d-56a9-83f4-b1c7ab4b5caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95076" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y},x=2t$ and $y={t}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}=4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2t)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}=4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =8t{e}^{{t}^{2}}+8{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1cab00d5-4da8-576c-bf80-814a66279e8a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360571 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06a85a62-20c8-53ea-9891-50994f57bf9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}(x-6)}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357811} {:answer/id #uuid "31bd0322-e103-58db-ab4e-d490d4079dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-1)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358194} {:answer/id #uuid "870209e6-e356-5edd-a169-1a95385cbde9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x(3x+4)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358933} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ec2678-1f3d-53f9-9edd-d1c6fda28115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{x(4x-3)}{x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359444} {:answer/id #uuid "d1925cff-23b1-5f65-a480-35f2ce80acbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359584}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186359584} :answer-field/id #uuid "a27a35b5-3297-5a09-b127-d1d744a50e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f411889e-b3c1-5aed-8247-b760065e6e98" :question/math-academy-id "q-95122" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ and $y={x}^{3},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y}{(x+1)^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{x+1}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}(x+1)-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}(2x+3)}{(x+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3a017487-80b3-5b03-a1d5-2934bfa2d036"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360555 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46f944b8-e799-5036-80f1-5ba5c1a7411f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358364} {:answer/id #uuid "82479d60-5437-50e5-882f-1cefac414e6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358893} {:answer/id #uuid "f1724fb0-eb83-58dc-8a3f-92689c7862c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359901} {:answer/id #uuid "fe84e3ca-9757-54b8-a1da-085a92705bae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359990} {:answer/id #uuid "fede68b1-836b-57e9-a83f-57bb56eb0c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359994}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358893} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef608987-b651-5ccb-b9c8-ffdc510994e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b0e7724b-5745-517d-9a70-23d66bdba953" :question/math-academy-id "q-95081" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x=s$ and $y=2s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=1+\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =1+\\frac{s}{2s}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s\\cdot 1+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e72f9a3-b2cc-52fb-b449-d5acf347acc1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360570 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dbcf33-c576-54cf-8c07-43b62cf69983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1+4{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358069} {:answer/id #uuid "558df00d-6a5e-5bb3-bb8a-e8288e5b1e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358497} {:answer/id #uuid "67861fb7-92d5-5f5e-8f74-f8ff3c7f315c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1-3{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358655} {:answer/id #uuid "70539894-ff4f-5b7d-809c-ae8c476c88fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358729} {:answer/id #uuid "da024232-5563-5f22-a044-1622a067232a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359659}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358729} :answer-field/id #uuid "f550e8b8-0dda-5ff3-ba5c-bfed41503899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5f396a56-19fc-58e7-855c-d3a310fbb9ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-95119" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fac2284d-62e5-5d58-b0c1-c75c972f080b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186524052}}] :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186523241}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199250} :activity/id #uuid "8416c2c4-cff0-5744-94c6-6f83c5fc5709" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050482 :topic/id #uuid "4d32e47e-9f37-5a31-95c8-7aa1fca70d66" :topic/math-academy-id 4132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199451 :tutorial/content "Suppose that \n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: (u,v) \\to \\left(x(u,v),y(u,v)\\right)\n$$\n\nis a $C^1$ transformation that has no critical points (i.e., it is invertible) inside some region $\\Delta\\subset \\mathbb R^2,$ and that $\\mathbf T(\\Delta) = D,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/66/668b8a0dae060c7929b92c6abe3601b0ede0b8444d92f33f4a8b5627410a4447.png)\n\n \n\nWe've already seen how the area of a non-trivial region $D$ can be computed using the change of variables formula:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nWe can extend this formula to compute the integral of a function $f(x,y)$ over $D,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d x \\mathrm d y = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v), y(u,v)) \\:  \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y)}{\\partial (u, v)} \\right|  \\ \\mathrm d u \\mathrm d v\n$$\n\nNote the similarity with the change of variables formula for the integral of a single-variable function $f(x)$ over $D = (a,b){:}$\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_D f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int\\limits_{\\Delta} f(x(u))\\,\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d u}\\, \\textrm d u\n$$\n\nApplying the change of variables formula is similar to when we were restricting our attention to areas in the plane. The only difference now is that we must also express the integrand $f(x,y)$ in terms of $u$ and $v.$\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "2fb63b1e-64c3-58e1-94d8-3d7a87445cd7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4402 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5a4dcbdc-b666-5ac5-82b5-d1e2d96f958e" :step/math-academy-id 26976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198361 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198235 :question/id #uuid "9d477733-7178-5b88-926f-a457faaf344a" :question/math-academy-id "e-7285"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fed8ac2-8d21-5bc9-b1cc-df20760cfbfb" :knowledge-po